{
  "image_name": "4af2cf106a305126c4b04348d95032bc.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a proposed link between SARS‑CoV‑2 infection, mucosal immune activation, and how different immunosuppressive therapies might influence COVID‑19 outcomes across three stages. The virus is shown infecting both the lung and intestine, apparently through ACE2‑expressing epithelial cells. Infection in these barrier tissues leads to mediator release and immune cell activation, with gut immune cells and dendritic cells contributing through homing pathways.\n\nFrom this shared early response, the diagram shows two possible downstream trajectories. One path favors controlled antiviral immunity, involving T helper, regulatory T, and B cells, which suppress excessive inflammation and lead to resolution. The other path progresses toward severe disease, characterized by T‑cell and B‑cell involvement together with TH17 cells and neutrophils, ending in cytokine storm syndrome and severe COVID‑19, with labels indicating neutrophilia, monocytopenia, and lymphopenia.\n\nDrug names are placed at different points to suggest where therapies may act. Vedolizumab appears to target gut immune cell trafficking, while ustekinumab and risankizumab are positioned near TH1 or TH17 pathways. A central box lists broader immunosuppressants such as azathioprine, MMF, tacrolimus, cyclosporine, and TNF blockers, implying they may influence the balance between resolution and severe inflammation.",
